  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Suppose $a<b$. Find a bijection $f$ from the interval $[0,1]$ to the interval $[a,b]$. What is the rule for the inverse of your bijection?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p38-q3-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "f(x)=(b-a)x+a,\\qquad f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-a}{b-a}",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p38-q3-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The positive slope makes the map injective, and every value in the target interval has a preimage.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Choose $f:[0,1]\\to[a,b]$ with $f(x)=(b-a)x+a$. Since $b-a>0$, the endpoint values $f(0)=a$ and $f(1)=b$ show that its values lie in $[a,b]$.\n\nIf $f(u)=f(v)$, then\n$$\\begin{align*}(b-a)u+a&=(b-a)v+a\\\\(b-a)u&=(b-a)v\\\\u&=v.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is injective.\n\nFor any $y\\in[a,b]$, take $x=\\frac{(y-a)}{(b-a)}$. Then $0\\le x\\le1$ and\n$$f\\left(\\frac{y-a}{b-a}\\right)=(b-a)\\frac{y-a}{b-a}+a=y.$$\nThus $f$ is surjective, so it is a bijection.\n\nTo find its inverse,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(b-a)x+a\\\\y-a&=(b-a)x\\\\\\frac{y-a}{b-a}&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $f^{-1}:[a,b]\\to[0,1]$ is given by $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{(x-a)}{(b-a)}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
